# --- asserted here; check before relying on it -------------------------------
200049|business partner of|200050|verify|McConnel and Kennedy ran the Ancoats firm together. The collection files both as factory owners in Ancoats without naming the partnership.||partner
200045|architect of|PL-BASE-026|verify|Alfred Waterhouse designed Strangeways prison and its assize courts as well as the town hall. The record gives only the town hall.||builtby
PL-BASE-013|named after|200036|verify|Peel Park in Salford was named for Sir Robert Peel, whose record is filed under Salford.||namedafter
PL-BASE-014|named after|200033|verify|Philips Park was named for Mark Philips, who campaigned and subscribed for it. The record spells the surname Phillips.||namedafter
PL-BASE-020|stands in|PL-BASE-013|verify|Salford Museum and Art Gallery stands in Peel Park. The collection keeps them as two separate records.||basedat
TL-BASE-001|returned him to Parliament|200033|verify|Mark Philips became one of Manchester's first two MPs at the election that followed the 1832 Reform Act.||tookpart
ORG-BASE-008|founded by|200020|verify|Benjamin Heywood was among the founders of the Manchester Statistical Society in 1833. The record has him only as an MP and philanthropist.||founded
PL-BASE-036|built by|ORG-BASE-001|verify|The Thirlmere aqueduct was built by Manchester Corporation to bring Lake District water to the city.||builtby
PL-BASE-031|traded on|PL-BASE-007|verify|Lewis's opened its Manchester store on Market Street in 1880.||traded
PL-BASE-046|joins the network at|PL-BASE-057|verify|The Ashton Canal met the Rochdale Canal at Ducie Street, on the Piccadilly basin.||nearby
PL-BASE-005|joins the network at|PL-BASE-057|verify|The Rochdale Canal runs through the Piccadilly basin on its way across the city.||nearby
PL-BASE-018|the other cathedral|PL-BASE-042|verify|St John's is the Roman Catholic cathedral of the Salford diocese; Manchester Cathedral is the Anglican one. The collection files both under Manchester.||nearby
200064|friend and biographer|200082|verify|Elizabeth Gaskell and Charlotte Bronte were friends, and Gaskell wrote her life. The collection records Bronte's Manchester stay but connects her to nobody.||collaborated
200089|field naturalist|COL-002|verify|Her record describes an ornithologist and polymath born in Salford; the collection leaves her unattached to any natural-history record.||member
200059|father of|VM-ADD-010|verify|William Fairbairn the engineer was the father of Thomas Fairbairn, who chaired the Art Treasures executive. The collection spelled the engineer's surname Fairburn; corrected here.||parentchild
200062|took to the poll by|200068|verify|Lily Maxwell voted in 1867 after Lydia Becker found her name on the register and took her to the poll; the Chorlton v Lings case followed.||introduced
200079|militant of|ORG-BASE-005|verify|Annie Kenney led WSPU organising out of Manchester; the collection records the suffrage society but connects her to nothing.||member
200076|organised with|ORG-BASE-004|verify|Hannah Mitchell's own record names the ILP and trade unions, which the collection does not resolve; the Women's Trades Council is its nearest body.||member
200022|food campaigner|ORG-BASE-014|verify|The Vegetarian Society, founded in Salford, is the collection's only food-reform body; her record calls her an artist and food campaigner without naming one.||member
