# --- readings offered for testing --------------------------------------------
PL-BASE-053|the same commercial quarter as|PL-BASE-016|interpretive|Bank, exchange and banking hall within a few streets of each other; the collection records the buildings but not the quarter they make.||nearby
PL-BASE-052|the same commercial quarter as|PL-BASE-053|interpretive|Two Edwardian commercial palaces built within a decade of each other, both now hotels.||nearby
PL-BASE-038|built in the same decade and manner as|PL-BASE-053|interpretive|Both records describe Edwardian Baroque buildings of the 1900s.||nearby
PL-BASE-043|the other side of the education argument from|ORG-BASE-007|interpretive|The Grammar School record calls it a historically male institution and part of broader education debates; the Ladies' Educational Association is the counterpart claim.||reading
PL-BASE-054|women's provision, like|PL-BASE-051|interpretive|One record claims the baths mattered for women's health and hygiene, the other that the settlement mattered for women's reform work. Neither names the other.||reading
200061|policed the same city as|200070|interpretive|Two Manchester police records a generation apart, one a constable who lived as a man, one the detective who raided a Hulme drag ball. The collection connects neither to a force.||reading
200075|a first in his sport, like|200080|interpretive|Both records mark a first for a black sportsman in England, in Tameside and Salford; the collection keeps them apart.||reading
200067|Salford religious welfare, like|ORG-BASE-015|interpretive|A Salvationist in Eccles and a Salford children's refuge, both 1870s religious welfare. Neither record names the other.||reading
200090|Salford's later voice|PL-BASE-020|interpretive|Delaney (1938-2011) falls outside the Victorian span the rest of the collection covers. Joined to Salford's public cultural institution so the record is reachable, and flagged here as out of period.||reading
