# --- following the Head of Botany's correction -----------------------------------
PL-OLDHAM|part of|COL-004|documented|A public museum holding private natural-history collections is what this heading collects.||partof
PL-BOLTON|part of|COL-004|documented|A public museum holding private natural-history collections is what this heading collects.||partof
PL-TAMESIDE|part of|COL-004|documented|A public museum holding private natural-history collections is what this heading collects.||partof
PL-OLDHAM|holds the collection of|COL-001|verify|The Head of Botany at Manchester Museum points to Gallery Oldham for Greater Manchester material. Oldham was one of the towns the Lancashire artisan botanists worked out of, so this is where to look for what they gathered - but nobody has yet matched a named collector to a sheet.|https://hla.oldham.gov.uk/gallery/collections/|heldat
PL-BOLTON|holds the collection of|COL-001|verify|Named by the Head of Botany at Manchester Museum as a local collection with local material. Which of the artisan botanists here are represented in it is an open question.|https://www.boltonlams.co.uk/collections|heldat
PL-TAMESIDE|holds the collection of|COL-001|verify|Named by the Head of Botany at Manchester Museum. Ashton and its neighbours were artisan-botanist country and the collecting grounds are on the doorstep.|https://www.tameside.gov.uk/librariesandleisure/museumsandgalleries/museums-and-galleries-collections-overview#nat|heldat
PL-TAMESIDE|collecting ground for|PL-NH-009|verify|Ashton Moss lies a mile from Ashton-under-Lyne, and the Tameside collections are the nearest repository to it.||collectedat
PL-OLDHAM|had as a member|ORG-NH-004|verify|The United Lancashire botanical societies met at Oldham among other towns; whether their material reached the town's museum is worth asking.||member
